  "hook_0_to_3s": "In this town, nothing grew on the hill, not grass, not weeds, nothing.",
  "text": "In this town, nothing grew on the hill, not grass, not weeds, nothing. Every seed anyone ever planted up there was dead in a week. So an eight-year-old Theo carried a tiny pine tree up that hill. The whole street laughed at him. Theo didn't argue. He just went home, got another seed, and planted that one too. That became his rule. Every time somebody laughed at him, he planted another tree. And the loudest laugh always came from Rosa, the girl next door. She laughed at every single one, always right when he was about to give up. At 12, Theo had 40 trees, and 39 of them were dead. But one was alive, nobody could explain it. Not even Theo. At 18, the whole school laughed when he skipped prom to carry buckets up the hill. That night, he planted six more. At 30, the town laughed when he spent his savings on baby trees. He planted every single one. Rosa laughed so hard she had to sit down. At 50, people stopped laughing. They just shook their heads. So he planted one for every head shake. And by 70, Theo's hill was the only green thing anyone could see from town. Then the storm came. It rained for three days straight. The river jumped the bridge. And on the third night, the hill above the town started to slide. Theo was up there alone, in the dark. Tying his youngest trees to six, so the water wouldn't rip them out. The mud was up to his knees. He slipped twice. He kept the time. Down below, everyone ran out into the street, staring up at that hill, waiting for the mud to come down on their houses. It never came. Thousands of roots were holding that hill together. The water just ran down slow and brown, between the trees and into the river. The next morning, the whole town climbed the hill. The same people who'd laughed at him for 60 years stood there in the mud and didn't say a word. Theo walked past all of them, up to the very first tree that ever lived. Leaning against it was an old rusty watering can. The same can that had been sitting there, full, every morning since he was 12. Scratched into the side was one word, Rosa.",
